@page "/mcp-server"

@* Describes the MCP server this demo's host maps at /mcp. Its route differs from the endpoint's on
   purpose: two literal endpoints on /mcp would make a GET of it ambiguous. *@

<PageTitle>MCP server - bit Bdnd</PageTitle>

<div class="demo-header">
    <h3>MCP server</h3>
    <p class="text-muted">
        This site hosts a Model Context Protocol server at <code>/mcp</code>, so an AI agent writing drag-and-drop
        code with Bit.Bdnd reads the library itself - its compiled API documentation, its guide, the components and
        these demo pages - instead of guessing at an API it half remembers, or borrowing one from React DnD. Every
        tool is read-only.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="mcp-panel">
    <h4>Connect a client</h4>

    <p>
        The endpoint is plain Streamable HTTP, so any MCP client connects with a URL and nothing else. The same tools
        are also served, version by version, through the bit platform's own server at
        <code>https://bitplatform.dev/mcp</code>, next to the other bit libraries.
    </p>

    <pre class="mcp-output"><code>@ClientConfig</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="mcp-panel">
    <h4>Tools</h4>

    <table class="mcp-table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it answers</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>SearchBdnd</code></td>
                <td>Everything at once - the guide, every public member, the setup guides and the demo's pages - each hit with the exact follow-up call.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GetBdndSetupGuide</code></td>
                <td>The complete wiring for one render mode: server, wasm, auto, standalone-wasm or hybrid - the service registration and the provider included.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GetBdndGuideSection</code></td>
                <td>One section of the reference guide, or the index of sections.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GetBdndApi</code></td>
                <td>One public type with every parameter, property and method, its real default and its documentation - or the index of types.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>InspectBdndDrop</code></td>
                <td>Runs a drag through the real components - a draggable and a tree of drop targets - and reports which targets can drop, which are hovered (deep and shallow), the order <code>OnDrop</code> runs in and what <code>OnEnd</code> receives - or a sample scenario, to see the format.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GetBdndSourceFile</code></td>
                <td>One demo or library file verbatim - every page here is a working example - or the index of files.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
        The guide, the API, a sample scenario and the sources are also exposed as <code>bdnd://</code>
        resources, and three prompts - <code>add-bdnd-to-app</code>, <code>implement-bdnd-feature</code> and
        <code>debug-bdnd-drag</code> - walk an agent through the common jobs in the right order.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="mcp-panel">
    <h4>The same tools over plain HTTP</h4>

    <p>
        Every tool is also a <code>GET</code> under <code>/api/mcp/...</code>: open one in a browser tab to read
        exactly what an agent is handed.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><a href="/api/mcp/SearchBdnd?query=sortable%20list" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/api/mcp/SearchBdnd?query=sortable list</a></li>
        <li><a href="/api/mcp/GetBdndApi?typeName=BdndDraggable" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/api/mcp/GetBdndApi?typeName=BdndDraggable</a></li>
        <li><a href="/api/mcp/InspectBdndDrop" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/api/mcp/InspectBdndDrop</a></li>
        <li><a href="/api/mcp/GetBdndSourceFile?path=Demo/Client/Pages/Sortable/SortableCard.razor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/api/mcp/GetBdndSourceFile?path=Demo/Client/Pages/Sortable/SortableCard.razor</a></li>
    </ul>
</section>

@code {
    private const string ClientConfig = """
        // .mcp.json (Claude Code, VS Code, and anything else reading this shape)
        {
          "mcpServers": {
            "bdnd": {
              "type": "http",
              "url": "https://bdnd.bitplatform.dev/mcp"
            }
          }
        }
        """;
}
